# Migration Guide

Learn how to successfully adopt Stricture in existing projects - from initial setup to team rollout.

## Migration Scenarios

<CardGrid>
  <Card title="From Nothing" icon="rocket">
    Adding architecture enforcement to a project without any.
  </Card>
  <Card title="From Other Tools" icon="swap">
    Migrating from dependency-cruiser, NX, or other tools.
  </Card>
  <Card title="Legacy Codebase" icon="document">
    Incremental adoption in large projects with violations.
  </Card>
  <Card title="Team Rollout" icon="group">
    Rolling out Stricture across your organization.
  </Card>
</CardGrid>

## Migrating from No Architecture Enforcement

### Phase 1: Assessment (Week 1)

Start by understanding your current architecture.

#### Map Your Existing Structure

```bash
# Visualize your directory structure
tree -d -L 3 src/
```

Document what you find:

```
src/
├── components/       # UI components (mixed concerns?)
├── services/        # Business logic + API calls
├── utils/           # Shared utilities
├── api/             # API layer
└── db/              # Database access
```

Ask these questions:

- **Is there an intended architecture?** (Even informal)
- **What layers exist?** (UI, business logic, data access)
- **What are the pain points?** (Circular dependencies, tight coupling)
- **Where do violations occur?** (Components calling DB directly?)

#### Analyze Import Patterns

Use a tool to understand current dependencies:

```bash
# Install madge to analyze dependencies
npm install -g madge

# Generate dependency graph
madge --image graph.svg src/

# Find circular dependencies
madge --circular src/
```

#### Choose an Architecture

Based on your codebase, pick a pattern:

| Architecture | Best For |
|--------------|----------|
| **Layered** | Traditional N-tier apps, REST APIs |
| **Hexagonal** | Domain-rich business logic, DDD |
| **Clean** | Complex enterprise applications |
| **Modular** | Feature-based, vertical slices |
| **Next.js** | Next.js App Router projects |

:::tip[Start Simple]
When in doubt, start with **Layered** architecture. It's the easiest to understand and implement.
:::

### Phase 2: Setup (Week 1-2)

Install Stricture with your chosen preset:

<Tabs>
  <TabItem label="npm">
    ```bash
    npm install -D @stricture/core @stricture/eslint-plugin @stricture/layered
    ```
  </TabItem>
  <TabItem label="pnpm">
    ```bash
    pnpm add -D @stricture/core @stricture/eslint-plugin @stricture/layered
    ```
  </TabItem>
  <TabItem label="yarn">
    ```bash
    yarn add -D @stricture/core @stricture/eslint-plugin @stricture/layered
    ```
  </TabItem>
</Tabs>

Create `.stricture/config.json`:

```json title=".stricture/config.json"
{
  "preset": "@stricture/layered"
}
```

Add to ESLint config:

<Tabs>
  <TabItem label="Flat Config">
    ```js title="eslint.config.js"
    import stricture from '@stricture/eslint-plugin';

    export default [
      {
        plugins: {
          '@stricture': stricture
        },
        rules: {
          '@stricture/enforce-boundaries': 'warn'  // Start with warnings
        }
      }
    ];
    ```
  </TabItem>
  <TabItem label="Legacy Config">
    ```js title=".eslintrc.js"
    module.exports = {
      plugins: ['@stricture'],
      rules: {
        '@stricture/enforce-boundaries': 'warn'  // Start with warnings
      }
    };
    ```
  </TabItem>
</Tabs>

:::note[Start with Warnings]
Use `'warn'` initially to avoid blocking builds. Upgrade to `'error'` once violations are fixed.
:::

### Phase 3: Map Current Structure to Preset (Week 2)

Your structure likely doesn't match the preset exactly. Customize the boundaries:

#### Example: Mapping to Layered Architecture

Your structure:

```
src/
├── components/       # UI layer
├── services/        # Business logic
├── api/             # API/HTTP layer
├── repositories/    # Data access
└── db/              # Database utilities
```

Custom configuration:

```json title=".stricture/config.json"
{
  "preset": "@stricture/layered",
  "boundaries": [
    {
      "name": "presentation",
      "pattern": "src/{components,pages}/**",
      "mode": "file",
      "tags": ["presentation"],
      "metadata": { "layer": 3 }
    },
    {
      "name": "business",
      "pattern": "src/services/**",
      "mode": "file",
      "tags": ["business"],
      "metadata": { "layer": 2 }
    },
    {
      "name": "data",
      "pattern": "src/{repositories,db,api}/**",
      "mode": "file",
      "tags": ["data"],
      "metadata": { "layer": 1 }
    }
  ],
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "presentation-to-business",
      "from": { "tag": "presentation" },
      "to": { "tag": "business" },
      "allowed": true
    },
    {
      "id": "business-to-data",
      "from": { "tag": "business" },
      "to": { "tag": "data" },
      "allowed": true
    },
    {
      "id": "no-skip-layers",
      "from": { "tag": "presentation" },
      "to": { "tag": "data" },
      "allowed": false,
      "message": "Presentation layer cannot directly access data layer. Use business layer."
    }
  ]
}
```

:::tip[Incremental Boundaries]
Start with broad boundaries (3-4 layers). Refine them as you fix violations.
:::

### Phase 4: Identify Violations (Week 2-3)

Run ESLint to find all violations:

```bash
# Get full report
npx eslint . --format stylish > violations.txt

# Count violations by type
npx eslint . --format json | jq '.[] | .messages | length' | awk '{sum+=$1} END {print sum}'
```

Categorize violations:

1. **Quick wins** - Easy to fix (wrong imports)
2. **Refactoring needed** - Require code changes
3. **Architecture violations** - Major structural issues

#### Create a Violation Dashboard

```bash
# Install ESLint HTML reporter
npm install -D eslint-html-reporter

# Generate HTML report
npx eslint . -f node_modules/eslint-html-reporter/reporter.js -o violations.html

# Open in browser
open violations.html
```

### Phase 5: Fix Violations (Week 3-8)

#### Strategy 1: File-by-File

Fix one file at a time:

```bash
# Fix a single file
npx eslint src/components/UserProfile.tsx --fix
```

#### Strategy 2: Layer-by-Layer

Fix violations starting from the innermost layer:

1. Fix data layer violations first
2. Then business layer
3. Finally presentation layer

#### Strategy 3: Incremental Rules

Enable rules gradually:

```json title=".stricture/config.json"
{
  "preset": "@stricture/layered",
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "no-skip-layers",
      "severity": "error"  // Enable this rule first
    },
    {
      "id": "business-isolation",
      "severity": "off"    // Enable later
    }
  ]
}
```

#### Common Fixes

**Violation: Presentation calling data directly**

```typescript
// ❌ Before
// src/components/UserProfile.tsx
import { UserRepository } from '../repositories/user-repository'

function UserProfile() {
  const user = await UserRepository.find(id)
  return <div>{user.name}</div>
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ After - Add business layer
// src/services/user-service.ts
export class UserService {
  constructor(private repo: UserRepository) {}

  async getUser(id: string) {
    return this.repo.find(id)
  }
}

// src/components/UserProfile.tsx
import { UserService } from '../services/user-service'

function UserProfile() {
  const user = await userService.getUser(id)
  return <div>{user.name}</div>
}
```

**Violation: Circular dependencies**

```typescript
// ❌ Before - Circular
// src/services/user-service.ts
import { OrderService } from './order-service'

// src/services/order-service.ts
import { UserService } from './user-service'
```

```typescript
// ✅ After - Extract shared logic
// src/services/shared/validation.ts
export const validateEmail = (email: string) => { ... }

// src/services/user-service.ts
import { validateEmail } from './shared/validation'

// src/services/order-service.ts
import { validateEmail } from './shared/validation'
```

### Phase 6: Team Rollout (Week 4+)

#### Communicate the Change

Send a team announcement:

```markdown
# Architecture Enforcement with Stricture

Starting next week, we're enforcing architecture boundaries with Stricture.

**What's changing:**
- ESLint will flag violations of our layered architecture
- CI builds will fail on architecture violations

**What you need to do:**
1. Run `npm install` to get the new dependencies
2. Run `npx eslint .` to check for violations in your branch
3. Fix any violations before merging

**Resources:**
- [Internal wiki: Our Architecture]
- [Stricture docs](https://stricture.dev)
- #architecture Slack channel for questions

**Rollout plan:**
- Week 1: Warnings only (CI passes)
- Week 2: Errors in CI (PRs blocked)
```

#### Training Session

Hold a 30-minute session:

1. **Why architecture matters** (5 min)
   - Maintenance cost
   - Onboarding time
   - Bug reduction

2. **Our architecture** (10 min)
   - Show the layers
   - Show the diagram
   - Explain the rules

3. **How Stricture works** (10 min)
   - Demo violations
   - Show error messages
   - Fix a violation live

4. **Q&A** (5 min)

#### Create Documentation

Add to your project README:

```markdown
## Architecture

This project uses a layered architecture enforced by Stricture:

```mermaid
graph TB
    Presentation --> Business
    Business --> Data
    style Presentation fill:#e3f2fd
    style Business fill:#f1f8e9
    style Data fill:#fff3e0
```

**Layers:**
- **Presentation** (`components/`, `pages/`) - UI components
- **Business** (`services/`) - Business logic
- **Data** (`repositories/`, `api/`) - Data access

**Rules:**
- Presentation can call Business
- Business can call Data
- No layer skipping (Presentation → Data is forbidden)
```

### Phase 7: CI Integration (Week 5+)

Add to CI pipeline:

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="GitHub Actions">
```yaml title=".github/workflows/ci.yml"
name: CI

on: [push, pull_request]

jobs:
  lint:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 20
      - run: npm ci
      - run: npm run lint  # Includes Stricture
```
</TabItem>
<TabItem label="GitLab CI">
```yaml title=".gitlab-ci.yml"
lint:
  image: node:20
  script:
    - npm ci
    - npm run lint
```
</TabItem>
<TabItem label="CircleCI">
```yaml title=".circleci/config.yml"
version: 2.1
jobs:
  lint:
    docker:
      - image: cimg/node:20.0
    steps:
      - checkout
      - run: npm ci
      - run: npm run lint
```
</TabItem>
</Tabs>

Upgrade severity to error:

```js title="eslint.config.js"
export default [
  {
    rules: {
      '@stricture/enforce-boundaries': 'error'  // Now blocks CI
    }
  }
];
```

## Migrating from Other Tools

### From dependency-cruiser

dependency-cruiser uses `.dependency-cruiser.js` with rules. Here's how to migrate:

#### dependency-cruiser config

```javascript title=".dependency-cruiser.js"
module.exports = {
  forbidden: [
    {
      name: 'no-circular',
      severity: 'error',
      from: {},
      to: { circular: true }
    },
    {
      name: 'no-orphans',
      severity: 'warn',
      from: { orphan: true },
      to: {}
    },
    {
      name: 'no-ui-to-db',
      from: { path: '^src/ui' },
      to: { path: '^src/db' }
    }
  ]
}
```

#### Equivalent Stricture config

```json title=".stricture/config.json"
{
  "boundaries": [
    {
      "name": "ui",
      "pattern": "src/ui/**",
      "mode": "file",
      "tags": ["ui"]
    },
    {
      "name": "business",
      "pattern": "src/business/**",
      "mode": "file",
      "tags": ["business"]
    },
    {
      "name": "db",
      "pattern": "src/db/**",
      "mode": "file",
      "tags": ["db"]
    }
  ],
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "ui-to-business",
      "from": { "tag": "ui" },
      "to": { "tag": "business" },
      "allowed": true
    },
    {
      "id": "business-to-db",
      "from": { "tag": "business" },
      "to": { "tag": "db" },
      "allowed": true
    },
    {
      "id": "no-ui-to-db",
      "from": { "tag": "ui" },
      "to": { "tag": "db" },
      "allowed": false,
      "message": "UI layer cannot directly access database. Use business layer."
    }
  ]
}
```

**Key differences:**

| Feature | dependency-cruiser | Stricture |
|---------|-------------------|-----------|
| **Circular deps** | Built-in detection | ESLint detects via other rules |
| **Orphans** | Built-in detection | Not applicable (ESLint/TS detect unused) |
| **Path matching** | Regex patterns | Glob patterns |
| **Rule logic** | Explicit forbidden | Deny-by-default + explicit allowed |

:::note[Circular Dependencies]
Stricture prevents architectural violations that often cause circular deps. Use ESLint's `import/no-cycle` rule for explicit circular dependency detection.
:::

### From NX Module Boundaries

NX uses tags in `project.json`. Here's the migration:

#### NX config

```json title="project.json"
{
  "tags": ["type:feature", "scope:admin"]
}
```

```json title=".eslintrc.json"
{
  "rules": {
    "@nrwl/nx/enforce-module-boundaries": [
      "error",
      {
        "allow": [],
        "depConstraints": [
          {
            "sourceTag": "type:ui",
            "onlyDependOnLibsWithTags": ["type:feature", "type:util"]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

#### Equivalent Stricture config

```json title=".stricture/config.json"
{
  "boundaries": [
    {
      "name": "ui",
      "pattern": "libs/ui/**",
      "mode": "file",
      "tags": ["ui"]
    },
    {
      "name": "feature",
      "pattern": "libs/feature/**",
      "mode": "file",
      "tags": ["feature"]
    },
    {
      "name": "util",
      "pattern": "libs/util/**",
      "mode": "file",
      "tags": ["util"]
    }
  ],
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "ui-to-feature",
      "from": { "tag": "ui" },
      "to": { "tag": "feature" },
      "allowed": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ui-to-util",
      "from": { "tag": "ui" },
      "to": { "tag": "util" },
      "allowed": true
    }
  ]
}
```

**Benefits over NX:**

- Works outside NX workspaces
- More flexible pattern matching
- Better error messages
- Architecture-focused (not project-focused)

## Incremental Adoption Strategy

For large codebases with many violations, use this strategy:

### 1. Allowlist Pattern

Start by blocking new violations while allowing existing ones:

```json title=".stricture/config.json"
{
  "boundaries": [ /* ... */ ],
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "ui-to-db",
      "from": { "tag": "ui" },
      "to": { "tag": "db" },
      "allowed": false
    }
  ],
  "exceptions": [
    {
      "rule": "ui-to-db",
      "files": [
        "src/ui/legacy/UserProfile.tsx",
        "src/ui/legacy/Dashboard.tsx"
      ],
      "reason": "Legacy code - will be refactored in Q2 2024"
    }
  ]
}
```

:::note[Exceptions are Not Built-in]
Stricture doesn't have built-in exceptions. Use ESLint comments for now:

```typescript
// eslint-disable-next-line @stricture/enforce-boundaries
import { Database } from '../db'  // TODO: Refactor in Q2
```
:::

### 2. Directory-by-Directory

Enable Stricture in specific directories:

```json title="eslint.config.js"
export default [
  {
    // Stricture enabled only in new code
    files: ['src/features/**/*.ts'],
    plugins: { '@stricture': stricture },
    rules: {
      '@stricture/enforce-boundaries': 'error'
    }
  },
  {
    // Legacy code - no Stricture yet
    files: ['src/legacy/**/*.ts'],
    rules: {
      '@stricture/enforce-boundaries': 'off'
    }
  }
];
```

Gradually expand coverage:

**Week 1-2:** `src/features/**`
**Week 3-4:** `src/services/**`
**Week 5-6:** `src/components/**`
**Week 7+:** `src/legacy/**`

### 3. Rule-by-Rule

Enable rules incrementally:

```json title=".stricture/config.json"
{
  "preset": "@stricture/layered",
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "no-skip-layers",
      "severity": "error"     // ✅ Enabled
    },
    {
      "id": "presentation-isolation",
      "severity": "warn"      // ⚠️  Warning only
    },
    {
      "id": "business-pure",
      "severity": "off"       // ❌ Disabled for now
    }
  ]
}
```

### 4. Team-by-Team

Roll out to teams gradually:

| Week | Team | Status |
|------|------|--------|
| 1-2 | Platform | Pilot (warnings) |
| 3-4 | Platform | Enforced (errors) |
| 5-6 | Product | Pilot |
| 7-8 | Product | Enforced |
| 9+ | All teams | Enforced |

## Dealing with Legacy Violations

### Prioritization Matrix

| Impact | Effort | Priority |
|--------|--------|----------|
| High | Low | 🔴 Fix immediately |
| High | High | 🟡 Schedule for sprint |
| Low | Low | 🟢 Fix when nearby |
| Low | High | ⚪ Add to backlog |

**High Impact Violations:**
- UI calling database directly
- Business logic in UI components
- Circular dependencies
- Security boundaries violated

**Low Impact Violations:**
- Minor layering violations
- Test code violations
- Utils/helpers in wrong layer

### Create a Refactoring Plan

Track violations in your issue tracker:

```markdown
**Epic: Architecture Cleanup**

- [ ] #123 Fix UserProfile direct DB access (High/Low)
- [ ] #124 Extract auth logic from UI (High/High)
- [ ] #125 Move utils to correct layer (Low/Low)
- [ ] #126 Refactor legacy admin panel (Low/High)
```

### The Strangler Fig Pattern

Gradually replace legacy code:

1. **Create new architecture** alongside old
2. **Route new features** to new architecture
3. **Migrate old features** incrementally
4. **Delete old code** once everything is migrated

```
src/
├── legacy/              # Old code (Stricture disabled)
│   └── user-profile/
└── features/            # New code (Stricture enforced)
    └── user-profile/    # Gradually migrated
```

## Team Rollout Best Practices

### Before Rollout

- [ ] **Get buy-in from leadership** - Explain ROI (maintenance cost reduction)
- [ ] **Identify champions** - 2-3 developers who support the change
- [ ] **Document the architecture** - Clear diagrams and rules
- [ ] **Create example PRs** - Show how to fix violations
- [ ] **Set up office hours** - Weekly Q&A session

### During Rollout

- [ ] **Start with warnings** - Don't block builds immediately
- [ ] **Pair programming sessions** - Help developers fix violations
- [ ] **Share progress** - Weekly update on violation count
- [ ] **Celebrate wins** - Recognize teams that achieve 100% compliance
- [ ] **Be flexible** - Adjust rules based on feedback

### After Rollout

- [ ] **Monitor adoption** - Track violation trends
- [ ] **Refine rules** - Adjust based on real usage
- [ ] **Update documentation** - Keep architecture docs current
- [ ] **Onboarding** - Add Stricture to new developer training

## Measuring Success

### Metrics to Track

**Violation Count**

```bash
# Track violations over time
npx eslint . --format json | jq '[.[] | .messages | length] | add' > metrics/violations-$(date +%Y-%m-%d).txt
```

**Technical Debt**

```bash
# Estimate refactoring time
# Each violation ≈ 15-30 minutes
violations=$(npx eslint . --format json | jq '[.[] | .messages | length] | add')
echo "Estimated hours: $((violations * 20 / 60))"
```

**Compliance by Module**

```bash
# Violations by directory
npx eslint src/ --format json | jq -r '.[] | .filePath' | xargs dirname | sort | uniq -c
```

### Success Criteria

- ✅ **Week 4:** 50% reduction in violations
- ✅ **Week 8:** All critical violations fixed
- ✅ **Week 12:** 100% compliance, errors in CI

## Common Migration Pitfalls

### Pitfall 1: Boiling the Ocean

**Problem:** Trying to fix everything at once

**Solution:** Start small, iterate

```bash
# ❌ Bad
npx eslint . --fix  # Breaks everything

# ✅ Good
npx eslint src/features/checkout --fix  # One feature at a time
```

### Pitfall 2: Wrong Architecture

**Problem:** Chosen preset doesn't match codebase

**Solution:** Customize boundaries to fit reality

Don't force a hexagonal architecture on a simple CRUD app. Use layered instead.

### Pitfall 3: Team Resistance

**Problem:** Developers see it as bureaucracy

**Solution:** Show the value

- Demonstrate how violations caused bugs
- Show time saved during onboarding
- Highlight reduced merge conflicts

### Pitfall 4: Configuration Churn

**Problem:** Constantly changing rules

**Solution:** Lock down config after initial setup

```json
// ✅ Good - Stable config
{
  "preset": "@stricture/layered",
  "boundaries": [ /* Locked down */ ]
}

// ❌ Bad - Constantly changing
{
  "boundaries": [ /* Different every week */ ]
}
```

### Pitfall 5: Ignoring Context

**Problem:** Enforcing rules blindly

**Solution:** Allow pragmatic exceptions

```typescript
// Sometimes you need to break rules (rarely!)
// eslint-disable-next-line @stricture/enforce-boundaries -- Migration deadline
import { OldApi } from '../legacy'
```

